														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3575328">
									

<p>This is well-written, and a unique concept, but I'm going to have to no-vote it, because it is indicative of what I see as a growing problem with the Fifthists. Namely, they've been kept so intentionally vague as to their methods and motivations as a GoI that they're being used as more and more of a McGuffin (if I'm using the term correctly); a throw-away reason for 'A Thing' to be anomalous, without having worry about expository details as to the why.</p>
<p>And that's not to imply that every use of them is lazy. It's more that including them, by their nature, means you don't have to explain what they are trying to accomplish, nor that it has to make any sense or serve any greater goal. Indeed, I appreciate Fifthist instances where authors DO include such detail.</p>

